 Greek philosopher  whose teacher Socrates was put to death  by the government of Athens for  allegedly corrupting the youth by  teaching them about views and  philosophies that were contrary to the  accepted beliefs of their society just  like kameena Socrates wanted the youth  to look beyond the conventional wisdom  established by society and for this he  was called a liar and a troublemaker in  his famous book of the Republic Plato  wants to explain how philosophers people  who think outside the box and question  conventional wisdom differ from other  people in order to do this Plato gives  us an allegory in which a group of  people have been chained to the wall of  a cave their entire lives and they are  chained in such a way that they are  always facing the same blank wall there  is a fire burning behind them that they  can't see and the light that the fire  creates projects shadows on the wall the  shadows are all that these people know  it is their entire world their reality  this is all they've ever known  so they accept it as the only reality  because they just don't know any better  the philosopher is like a prisoner who  manages to free himself from the chains  and is able to stand up and see what's  really happening he comes to understand  that the shadows on the wall are not  reality just illusions projections and  eventually he is able to climb up and  leave the cave to see what the surface  really looks like the trees the sky the  Sun and so on the philosopher realizes  that everything that he has been seeing  inside the cave up to this point was  just a pale reflection of the real truth  a tiny fraction of a much greater  reality the philosopher then walks back  into the cave and tries to tell the  people inside the cave who are still  tied up in the same place that they are  just watching shadows on the wall not  the real world but the people who were  stuck in the cave don't believe him  because he is challenging everything  they think they know in